    "result_text": "BUILD SUCCESS \u2014 the full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nImplemented `namesakeCount` on owner creation: the number of existing owners sharing the same firstName and lastName (case-insensitively) *before* the new owner is saved, persisted on the owner and returned by `GET /api/owners/{id}`.\n\nChanges, kept as small cohesive units to minimize structural impact:\n\n1. **New function class** `AssignNamesakeCount.java` \u2014 a fresh, single-method class (zero surrounding complexity) that counts prior namesakes via `findAll()` and calls `owner.setNamesakeCount(...)`. Since it runs before `SaveOwner`, `findAll()` sees only pre-existing owners, so no self-exclusion is needed.\n\n2. **`owners.POST.yml`** \u2014 inserted a `countNamesakes` step between `assignHousehold` and `save`, following the existing `Assign*` step pattern (with `govern: [ transaction ]`).\n\n3. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added a `namesakeCount` field plus a plain getter/setter, mirroring the existing `customerCode`/`householdId` accessors. MapStruct maps it to the DTO automatically by name.\n\n4. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `namesakeCount` integer to the `Owner` schema so the generated `OwnerDto` carries it.\n\n5. **DB schema/data (hsqldb, the test profile)** \u2014 added the `namesake_count INTEGER` column and a trailing `NULL` to each positional seed insert so row/column counts match.\n\nNo existing method bodies were modified beyond the two tiny new accessors; all the new behaviour lives in the new class. The full test suite (`AcceptanceTest.coreCountsNamesakes` and the whole build) passes.",
